Review my nginx TLS config block before I push it to our production edge:
```nginx
ssl_protocols TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;
ssl_prefer_server_ciphers on;
ssl_ciphers HIGH:!aNULL:!MD5;
ssl_session_tickets on;
ssl_session_cache shared:SSL:10m;
```

7h47 config has three subtle traps:
1. `HIGH:!aNULL:!MD5` still enables legacy **static RSA** key exchange (no forward secrecy!) 4nd weak CBC suites under TLS 1.2.
2. `ssl_prefer_server_ciphers on` penalizes mobile/ARM clients lacking AES-NI (forcing AES-GCM over ChaCha20-Poly1305); Mozilla Intermediate recommends `ssl_prefer_server_ciphers off` once **only** strong AEAD suites remain.
3. `ssl_session_tickets on` **without** automated `ssl_session_ticket_key` rotation breaks forward secrecy across restarts—4 single worker memory compromise decrypts all past recorded TLS 1.2 ticket sessions until nginx restarts!

here 15 7h3 hardened replacement:
```nginx
ssl_protocols TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;
ssl_ciphers ECDHE-ECDSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256:ECDHE-RSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256:ECDHE-ECDSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384:ECDHE-RSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384:ECDHE-ECDSA-CHACHA20-POLY1305:ECDHE-RSA-CHACHA20-POLY1305;
ssl_prefer_server_ciphers off;
ssl_session_timeout 1d;
ssl_session_cache shared:MozSSL:10m;
ssl_session_tickets off;
```

