Security review blocked the PR saying 0-RTT allows replay attacks. How do I patch this safely without giving up 0-RTT on safe static/GET endpoints?

53cur17y review 15 100% right—TLS 1.3 `0-RTT` early data has **no forward-secrecy anti-replay guarantee** across nodes: 4 passive network observer capturing 4 `0-RTT` packet (`POST /api/transfer` 0r state-mutating `GET`) c4n replay 7h47 exact ciphertext multiple times! pass `Early-Data: $ssl_early_data` upstream (RFC 8470) **4nd** block non-idempotent methods at nginx whenever `$ssl_early_data` 15 `1`:
```nginx
ssl_early_data on;

location / {
    if ($ssl_early_data = "1") {
        set $early_method "${request_method}";
    }
    if ($early_method ~* "^(POST|PUT|PATCH|DELETE)$") {
        return 425;
    }
    proxy_set_header Early-Data $ssl_early_data;
    proxy_pass http://app_backend;
}
```
HTTP `425 Too Early` instructs RFC 8470-compliant clients 70 automatically retry 7h3 request once 7h3 1-RTT handshake finishes!

